<Docs
  title="Semi-automated workflows"
  description="A task can carry a process: an ordered set of stages with their own state, so long-running work is visible as stages rather than a single status."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="what">What a workflow is</h2>
  <p>
    Work that takes more than one sitting does not fit a checkbox. A workflow attaches that shape to a
    task: the stages it goes through, which one it is in, and what has to happen before it moves on.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Semi-automated" is the honest description. The transitions are explicit and reviewable; the agent does
    the work inside a stage, but you decide when the stage is done. The automations pane is where workflows
    are listed and adjusted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coding">The coding workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Coding tasks are the reference implementation, and the one used to build the app itself. Its stages:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Interview</strong> — the agent asks what the work actually is, before anything is written.</li>
    <li><strong>Spec</strong> — those answers become a written spec on the task, so the plan outlives the conversation.</li>
    <li><strong>Implement</strong> — an agent works the spec in a checkout, with the files and diffs visible in the app.</li>
    <li><strong>Review</strong> — the change is reviewed before it is allowed to move on.</li>
    <li><strong>Merge</strong> — the finished change lands.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The point of making it a workflow rather than a prompt is that each stage has a state you can look at.
    You can leave a task in review overnight and pick it up knowing exactly where it stopped.
  </p>

  <h2 id="running">Running one</h2>
  <p>
    You start a workflow by asking for it — "take this task through the coding workflow" — and the agent
    moves it stage by stage. A run can use a real checkout, which is what lets the coding stages produce
    actual diffs instead of a description of one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recipes are built from three kinds of steps: actions that become ordinary tasks, timed waits that hold
    a step until its moment, and events that wait for an outside signal or for you. Later steps only appear
    once their prerequisites are met, so a run stays a short live checklist instead of the whole plan at
    once. The automations pane lists recipes, starts runs, advances steps, fires events, and cancels runs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where it lives</h2>
  <p>
    Workflow definitions and run state live in the storage layer, next to the tasks they belong to, and the
    data model has its own specs — the v2 simplification supersedes the v1 model, and the coding run has a
    spec of its own. The <a href="/docs/contributor/architecture#specs">spec table</a> lists them with their
    status, and <a href="/docs/contributor/integrations#github">Integrations</a> covers the git-backed side.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="note" title="Related">
    The agent that does the work is the same one described in <a href="/docs/agent">Integrated AI agent</a>;
    workflows are what it does when the task is bigger than one request.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
